        "url": "https://bretcontreras.com/wp-content/uploads/An-electromyographic-comparison-of-a-modified-version-of-the-plank-with-a-long-lever-and-posterior-tilt-versus-the-traditional-plank-exercise.pdf"
      },
      {
        "id": "escamilla2006",
        "title": "Escamilla et al. 2006, electromyographic analysis of traditional and nontraditional abdominal exercises: implications for rehabilitation and training (Phys Ther 86(5):656-671; PubMed 16649890)",
        "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16649890/"
      }
    ],
    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG.",
        "Advanced: needs a solid hollow hold and long-lever plank first.",
        "Never put the body weight on the neck; stop if you feel neck or back pain.",
        "Holding the breath under effort raises blood pressure: breathe out slowly as you lower."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Tuck the knees (tuck dragon flag) to shorten the lever.",
        "One leg bent, one straight.",
        "Lower only partway."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Tuck negative dragon flag.",
        "Hollow hold.",
        "Lying leg raise on the bench, gripping behind the head."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Slower negatives with pauses.",
        "Full dragon flag (raise and lower straight)."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "core": "Rectus abdominis and the deep abdominals hold the body in one rigid line while it lowers as a long lever pivoting on the upper back: one of the hardest anti-extension holds, worked eccentrically here (clinical opinion; long-lever, posterior-tilt planks raise abdominal activity, Schoenfeld 2014).",
      "obliques_r": "Brace the trunk with rectus abdominis (clinical opinion).",
      "obliques_l": "Brace the trunk with rectus abdominis (clinical opinion).",
      "lats_r": "Pull down on the bench to pin the upper back as the pivot (clinical opinion).",
      "lats_l": "Pull down on the bench to pin the upper back as the pivot (clinical opinion).",
      "hip_flexors_r": "Stop the hips sagging into an arch as the legs lower (clinical opinion).",
      "hip_flexors_l": "Stop the hips sagging into an arch as the legs lower (clinical opinion).",
      "glute_max_r": "Keep the hips straight so the body does not pike (clinical opinion).",
      "glute_max_l": "Keep the hips straight so the body does not pike (clinical opinion).",
      "quads_r": "Keep the knees locked (clinical opinion).",
      "quads_l": "Keep the knees locked (clinical opinion).",
      "forearm_flexors_r": "Grip the bench behind the head (clinical opinion).",
      "forearm_flexors_l": "Grip the bench behind the head (clinical opinion).",
      "triceps_r": "The long head helps the lats hold the arms against the bench (clinical opinion).",
      "triceps_l": "The long head helps the lats hold the arms against the bench (clinical opinion)."
    }
  },
